00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.300
Cerebro spinal fluid waves that flush brain waves shrink markedly in older adults during deep sleep. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 243. How slow waves move fluid? Tremela hydrocaloids in worms, and gene therapy holding at 4 years follow. MIT researchers, according to MIT Health, scanned adults aged 18-39 and 60-84 during sleep with electroencephalography, or EEG, and specialized magnetic resonance imaging,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:36.120
or MRI. During non-REM sleep, slow waves ordinarily push larger pulses of cerebrospinal fluid

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.560
that clear debris. Sleep length was comparable across groups, yet the older adults still

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.880
generated smaller fluid pulses. Reduced neurovascular coupling accompanied that change,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.720
and blood flow responses to breath-holding tasks were weaker in the same older adults.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.640
The team plans to test whether boosting cerebrospinal fluid flow could alter neurodegenerative risk.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.589
Most people assume deep sleep is for topping up energy and filing memories, yet those same

00:01:03.589 --> 00:01:09.509
slow oscillations also drive rhythmic pulses of cerebrospinal fluid that wash metabolic debris

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:10.549
out of brain tissue.

00:01:11.169 --> 00:01:17.029
What the body is doing is tightly timed on each large low frequency EEG wave, blood volume

00:01:17.029 --> 00:01:20.849
falls a measurable amount, then cerebrospinal fluid rushes in.

00:01:21.389 --> 00:01:26.289
That incoming fluid carries waste toward venous drainage, so the electrical wave, the

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:31.789
blood volume drop, and the fluid surge are one coupled sequence rather than separate nighttime events.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.589
The MIT comparison was run under identical scanning conditions in the 60 to 84 group,

00:01:38.089 --> 00:01:43.989
and the 18 to 39 group, an average cerebrospinal fluid wave amplitude drops substantially in

00:01:43.989 --> 00:01:50.349
the older adults. Sustained waste accumulation is one hypothesized contributor to later neurodegeneration.

00:01:50.889 --> 00:01:55.529
Consistent schedules, light exposure timing, and treatment of sleep apnea are the

00:01:55.529 --> 00:01:59.309
supports tied to the deep sleep architecture that runs this fluid clearance.

